    "description": {
      "value": "<p><em>Wondrous Item, Artifact (Requires Attunement)</em></p><p>Long ago, in the Dragonlance setting, elves and humans fought a terrible war against chromatic dragons. When the world seemed doomed, the wizards of the Towers of High Sorcery joined forces and forged five <em>Orbs of Dragonkind</em> to help defeat the dragons. Each of the five towers received one, and there the orbs hastened the war toward victory: the wizards used them to lure dragons, then destroyed the dragons with powerful magic.</p><p>As the Towers of High Sorcery fell in later ages, the orbs were destroyed or faded into legend; only three are thought to survive. Their magic has warped over the centuries: they still call dragons, but they also grant some measure of control over them.</p><p>Each orb holds the essence of an evil dragon, a presence that resents any attempt to draw magic from it. Those who try to wield an orb’s magic without enough force of personality might find themselves under its control.</p><p>An orb is an etched crystal globe about 10 inches across. When used, it grows to about 20 inches across, and mist swirls inside it.</p><p>While attuned to an orb, you can take a Magic action to peer into its depths. You must then make a DC 15 Charisma saving throw. On a successful save, you control the orb for as long as you remain attuned to it. On a failed save, the orb gives you the &amp;Reference[Charmed apply=false] condition for as long as you remain attuned to it.</p><p>While the orb has you Charmed, you can’t voluntarily end your Attunement to it, and it casts @UUID[Compendium.dnd5e.spells24.Item.phbsplSuggestion]{Suggestion} on you at will (save DC 18), urging you to work toward the evil ends it desires. The dragon essence inside might want many things: the destruction of a particular society or organization, freedom from the orb, to spread suffering in the world, to advance the worship of Tiamat, or something else the DM decides.</p><p><strong>Random Properties.</strong> An <em>Orb of Dragonkind</em> has the following random properties (see “Artifacts”):</p><ul><li><p>2 minor beneficial properties</p></li><li><p>1 minor detrimental property</p></li><li><p>1 major detrimental property</p></li></ul><p><strong>Spells.</strong> The orb has 7 charges and regains 1d4 + 3 expended charges daily at dawn. If you control the orb, you can cast one of the spells on the following table from it, expending the number of charges it shows.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Spell</th><th>Charge Cost</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>@UUID[Compendium.dnd5e.spells24.Item.phbsplCureWounds]{Cure Wounds} (level 9 version)</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>@UUID[Compendium.dnd5e.spells24.Item.phbsplDaylight00]{Daylight}</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>@UUID[Compendium.dnd5e.spells24.Item.phbsplDeathWard0]{Death Ward}</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>@UUID[Compendium.dnd5e.spells24.Item.phbsplDetectMagi]{Detect Magic}</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>@UUID[Compendium.dnd5e.spells24.Item.phbsplScrying000]{Scrying} (save DC 18)</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Call Dragons.</strong> While you control the orb, you can take a Magic action to make it send out a telepathic call in all directions for 40 miles. Chromatic dragons in range feel compelled to come to the orb as quickly as possible by the most direct route; dragon deities such as Tiamat are unaffected. Dragons drawn this way might be Hostile toward you for compelling them against their will. Once used, this property can’t be used again for 1 hour.</p><p><strong>Destroying an Orb.</strong> An <em>Orb of Dragonkind</em> has AC 20 and is destroyed if it takes damage from a @UUID[Compendium.dnd5e.equipment24.Item.dmgWeapon12or300]{+3 Weapon} or a @UUID[Compendium.dnd5e.spells24.Item.phbsplDisintegra]{Disintegrate} spell. Nothing else can harm it.</p><section class=\"secret\" id=\"secret-cSxYFkggG1bDJVeG\"><p><strong>Foundry Note</strong></p><p>Les propriétés aléatoires d’artefact sont à ajouter par le MJ. dnd5e propose cet orbe sous le nom SRD <em>Dragon Orb</em>, sans son historique ni ses propriétés aléatoires.</p></section>",
      "chat": ""
    },
